1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
LIZZIE: So how are you getting on with your teaching practice at the High School, Joe?

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,880
JOE: Well I've been put in charge of the school marching band

3
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:14,018
and it's quite a responsibility.

4
00:00:14,018 --> 00:00:16,850
I'd like to talk it over with you.

5
00:00:16,850 --> 00:00:22,000
LIZZIE: Go ahead. You'd better start by giving me a bit of background.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:29,139
JOE: OK. Well the band has students in it from all years, so they're aged 11 to 18,

7
00:00:29,139 --> 00:00:33,180
and there are about 50 of them altogether

8
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:35,484
It's quite a popular activity within the school.

9
00:00:35,484 --> 00:00:41,830
I've never worked with a band of more than 20 before, and this is very different.

10
00:00:41,830 --> 00:00:44,100
LIZZIE: I can imagine.

11
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:48,196
JOE: They aren't really good enough to enter national band competitions,

12
00:00:48,196 --> 00:00:51,919
but they're in a regional one later in the term.

13
00:00:51,919 --> 00:00:53,781
Even if they don't win,

14
00:00:53,781 --> 00:00:56,120
and I don't expect them to

15
00:00:56,400 --> 00:01:00,110
hopefully it'll be an incentive for them to try and improve.

16
00:01:00,110 --> 00:01:02,860
LIZZIE: Yes, hopefully.

17
00:01:02,860 --> 00:01:08,340
JOE: Well, now the town council's organising a carnival in the summer

18
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:09,972
and the band has been asked to perform.

19
00:01:09,972 --> 00:01:13,884
If you ask me, they aren't really up to it yet,

20
00:01:13,884 --> 00:01:19,930
and I need to get them functioning better as a band, and in a very short time.

21
00:01:19,930 --> 00:01:22,780
LIZZIE: Have you been doing anything with them

22
00:01:23,140 --> 00:01:25,810
Apart from practising the music, I mean.

23
00:01:25,810 --> 00:01:28,440
JOE: I played a recording I came across

24
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:33,479
of a drummer talking about how playing in a band had changed his life.

25
00:01:33,479 --> 00:01:35,919
I think it was an after-dinner speech.

26
00:01:35,919 --> 00:01:37,980
I thought it was pretty inspiring

27
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:42,541
because being in the band had stopped him from getting involved in crime.

28
00:01:42,541 --> 00:01:45,330
The students seemed to find it interesting, too.

29
00:01:45,330 --> 00:01:47,080
LIZZIE: That's good.

30
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:52,959
JOE: I'm planning to show them that old film from the 1940s 'Strike Up the Band',

31
00:01:52,959 --> 00:01:57,000
and talk about it with the students. What do you think?

32
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,544
LIZZIE: Good idea. As it's about a school band,

33
00:02:00,544 --> 00:02:06,450
it might make the students realise how much they can achieve if they work together.

34
00:02:06,450 --> 00:02:08,624
JOE: That's what I've got in mind.

35
00:02:08,624 --> 00:02:14,523
I'm hoping I can take some of the band to a parade that's going to take place next month.

36
00:02:14,523 --> 00:02:17,420
A couple of marching bands will be performing

37
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:19,180
and the atmosphere should be quite exciting.

38
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:25,080
It depends on whether I can persuade the school to hire a coach or two to take us there.

39
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:28,690
LIZZIE: Mmm. They sound like good ideas to me.

40
00:02:28,690 --> 00:02:30,790
JOE: Thanks.

41
00:02:30,790 --> 00:02:34,060
JOE: Can I tell you about a few people in the band

42
00:02:34,060 --> 00:02:36,939
who I'm finding it quite difficult to cope with?

43
00:02:36,939 --> 00:02:40,160
I'm sure you'll have some ideas about what I can do.

44
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:41,800
LIZZIE: Go ahead.

45
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:46,059
JOE: There's a flautist who says she loves playing in the band.

46
00:02:46,059 --> 00:02:50,318
We rehearse twice a week after school, but she's hardly ever there.

47
00:02:50,318 --> 00:02:55,642
Then she looks for me the next day and gives me a very plausible reason -

48
00:02:55,642 --> 00:02:59,840
she says she had to help her mother, or she's been ill

49
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:02,740
but to be honest, I don't believe her.

50
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:08,140
LIZZIE: Oh dear! Any more students with difficulties?

51
00:03:08,140 --> 00:03:15,599
JOE: Plenty! There's a trumpeter who thinks she's the best musician in the band, though she certainly isn't.

52
00:03:15,599 --> 00:03:19,660
She's always saying what she thinks other people should do

53
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:22,230
which makes my job pretty difficult.

54
00:03:22,230 --> 00:03:25,210
LIZZIE: She sounds a bit of a nightmare!

55
00:03:25,210 --> 00:03:27,360
JOE: You can say that again.

56
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:31,420
One of the trombonists has got an impressive sense of rhythm

57
00:03:31,660 --> 00:03:33,453
and could be an excellent musician -

58
00:03:33,453 --> 00:03:36,060
except that he has breathing difficulties

59
00:03:36,280 --> 00:03:39,188
and he doesn't really have enough breath for the trombone.

60
00:03:39,188 --> 00:03:44,564
He'd be much better off playing percussion, for instance, but he refuses to give up.

61
00:03:44,564 --> 00:03:47,790
So he ends up only playing half the notes.

62
00:03:47,790 --> 00:03:52,050
LIZZIE: I suppose you have to admire his determination.

63
00:03:52,050 --> 00:03:56,620
JOE: Hmm, maybe. One of the percussionists isn't too bad

64
00:03:57,100 --> 00:03:58,664
but he never seems to interact with other people,

65
00:03:58,664 --> 00:04:02,840
and he always rushes off as soon as the rehearsal ends

66
00:04:03,620 --> 00:04:05,974
I don't know if there are family reasons, or what.

67
00:04:05,974 --> 00:04:08,600
But it isn't good in a band

68
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:12,240
where people really need to feel they're part of a group.

69
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:14,390
LIZZIE: Hmm.

70
00:04:14,390 --> 00:04:19,790
JOE: There are others too, but at least that gives you an idea of what I'm up against.

71
00:04:19,790 --> 00:04:23,090
Do you have any thoughts about what I can do, Lizzie?
